    "body": "\n\n## Overview\nAdds `VLLM_ROCM_AITER_MLA_USE_FP8_PREFILL` (default `true`). Setting it to `0` disables the gfx950 FP8 asm MLA prefill, so prefill runs through the bf16 flash-attention path instead. Default behavior is unchanged.\n\n## Claims\n- Opt-out only; FP8 asm prefill stays the default where supported.\n- Kimi-K2.6-MXFP4 TP8 on MI355X: the bf16 path is ~8-9% faster per prefill step and ~8.6% lower TTFT, with equal GSM8K accuracy.\n- End-to-end latency is not a reliable discriminator at 1,024 output tokens; see the note below.\n\n## Validation\nKimi-K2.6-MXFP4, TP8, MI355X. `vllm bench serve`, random 1024/1024, 64 prompts, concurrency 64, `--ignore-eos --temperature 0 --request-rate inf`.\n\nClient-side, no profiler attached:\n\n| Metric | FP8 asm prefill (default) | bf16 (`VLLM_ROCM_AITER_MLA_USE_FP8_PREFILL=0`) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Mean TTFT | 934.70 ms | 854.10 ms (−8.6%) |\n| Mean TPOT | 14.80 ms | 14.50 ms |\n| Mean E2EL | 16,079.61 ms | 15,684.85 ms |\n| Total token throughput | 8,144.80 tok/s | 8,347.46 tok/s |\n\nSame workload under the torch profiler, rank-0 kernel sums:\n\n| Metric | FP8 asm prefill | bf16 |\n|---|---|---|\n| Prefill step, 31,745 prompt tokens + 32 decode rows | 531.9 ms | 489.0 ms (−8.1%) |\n| Prefill attention core, per layer | ~880 µs | ~155 µs |\n| Prefill MoE layer total | 8,701 µs | 8,005 µs |\n| Decode step, average | 14.45 ms | 14.71 ms |\n| Mean TTFT | 954.7 ms | 873.7 ms |\n\nGSM8K 5-shot, flexible / strict:\n\n| FP8 asm prefill | bf16 |\n|---|---|\n| 0.9333 / 0.9333 | 0.9303 / 0.9310 |\n\n**On end-to-end numbers.** The prefill difference is ~81 ms of a ~15.7 s request at 1,024 output tokens, about 0.5% of E2EL, and it reproduces across runs (+80.6 ms and +81.0 ms for FP8 in two separate runs). TPOT does not reproduce: it came out 0.30 ms against FP8 in the run above and 0.24 ms in its favor in the profiled run, a ~2% swing either way. That swing lives entirely in the decode MoE GEMMs — the two paths generate different (equally valid) tokens, so each decode step touches a different number of experts. Since the decode noise is roughly four times the prefill share of E2EL, the E2EL column can flip sign between runs and should not be read as a property of the prefill path. TTFT and the per-step prefill kernel sums are the numbers that isolate this change.\n\n## Details\nAt 8 heads per rank the FP8 path pads q/k/v to 16 heads (~355 µs), casts them to fp8 (~156 µs), and runs the asm kernel plus its reduce (~345 µs) and the copies back; flash attention does the same work in ~155 µs. FP8 prefill was added to avoid the bf16 working set at very long context (#51040), so it stays the default and this only provides a way to opt out on shapes where the padding overhead dominates.\n\nNo duplicate: no open issue or PR adds a way to turn off the FP8 prefill path.\n\nAI assistance was used to prepare this change and description; I reviewed every changed line.\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary> Pull Request Checklist </summary>\n\n- [ ] I used vLLM's `/pr-checklist` skill. (Mandatory for agents, optional for humans).\n- [X] AI assistance was used during the creation of this PR.\n\n- [ ] **Design Fit:** Minimizes impact on core components, reuses existing functionality, and justifies added complexity.\n- [ ] **Testing and Validation:** Validates the change and ensures any added tests are meaningful and reliable, with CI coverage or documented CI resource constraints and validation performed outside CI.\n- [ ] **Code Quality and Style:** Keeps code and comments clear and concise, and updates relevant documentation and examples.\n- [ ] **Pull Request Contents:** Includes a brief summary and relevant links, supports claims with evidence, explains root causes and implementation trade-offs, and follows the contributing guide.\n</details>\n\n",
